# Per-sample MAE distribution

**What this is.** For every sample in the cohort and every
comparison tool, the mean absolute error between popout's
proportion vector and the tool's vector (units 0–1, averaged over
the SP6 components). The violin shows `log10(MAE)` so the long
tail compresses; the table reports the actual MAE quantiles.

**How to read this chart.** A violin sitting near `log10(0.01)`
(~ -2) means typical disagreement of 1 percentage point — the
tools largely agree. A violin shifted right toward
`log10(0.1)` (~ -1) means typical 10pp disagreement; that level
of drift is hard to call "agreement" no matter what Pearson r
says. The width of the violin tells you how heterogeneous the
disagreement is across the cohort.

